oblem" "value" "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix with eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1$ corresponding to the eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ respectively, where\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSolve the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/state.json" "file_sha256" "83532cd8775da02f680d928be9e2f1005c5182bcc3e6f71ba99792efe1d70ef7"} {"question" "q-39240" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ has two distinct eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1},{\\lambda }_{2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2},$ the formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our matrix differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$, we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2} \\\\ -2{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}=3 \\\\ -2{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $2$ and adding the result to the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n6{c}_{2}=3\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=3\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/state.json" "file_sha256" "83532cd8775da02f680d928be9e2f1005c5182bcc3e6f71ba99792efe1d70ef7"} {"question" "q-39240" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "89ff37eb35ec8c1ba12426d1ac9dd8c7ed420a07f95af35c6e904141d57a9a35"} {"question" "q-39240" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/state.json" "file_sha256" "83532cd8775da02f680d928be9e2f1005c5182bcc3e6f71ba99792efe1d70ef7"} {"question" "q-39240" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}"] ["math" "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}"] ["math" "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}"] ["math" "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}"] ["math" "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/state.json" "file_sha256" "83532cd8775da02f680d928be9e2f1005c5182bcc3e6f71ba99792efe1d70ef7"} {"question" "q-39240" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/state.json" "file_sha256" "83532cd8775da02f680d928be9e2f1005c5182bcc3e6f71ba99792efe1d70ef7"} {"question" "q-327160" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-4{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/state.json" "file_sha256" "83532cd8775da02f680d928be9e2f1005c5182bcc3e6f71ba99792efe1d70ef7"} {"question" "q-327160" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write down the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for the given system:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A.$\n\n- We are given that the eigenvalues are\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=1\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-2.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1/4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 4\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2(0)} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ 4{c}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ 4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=2 \\\\ 4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the first equation from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{c}_{1}=-3\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${c}_{1}=-1$ into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1+{c}_{2}=2\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/state.json" "file_sha256" "83532cd8775da02f680d928be9e2f1005c5182bcc3e6f71ba99792efe1d70ef7"} {"question" "q-327160" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "89ff37eb35ec8c1ba12426d1ac9dd8c7ed420a07f95af35c6e904141d57a9a35"} {"question" "q-327160" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/state.json" "file_sha256" "83532cd8775da02f680d928be9e2f1005c5182bcc3e6f71ba99792efe1d70ef7"} {"question" "q-327160" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x(t)=-2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+4[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}"] ["math" "x(t)=-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}"] ["math" "x(t)=2[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}-4[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}"] ["math" "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}"] ["math" "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/state.json" "file_sha256" "83532cd8775da02f680d928be9e2f1005c5182bcc3e6f71ba99792efe1d70ef7"} {"question" "q-327160" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x(t)=-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/state.json" "file_sha256" "83532cd8775da02f680d928be9e2f1005c5182bcc3e6f71ba99792efe1d70ef7"} {"question" "q-326437" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/state.json" "file_sha256" "83532cd8775da02f680d928be9e2f1005c5182bcc3e6f71ba99792efe1d70ef7"} {"question" "q-326437" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write down the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for the given system:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A.$\n\n- Since $A$ is lower-triangular, the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=1\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=3.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{c}_{1}=1 \\\\ {c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get ${c}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Substituting into the second equation, we obtain ${c}_{2}=-\\frac{7}{2}.$ \n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}-\\frac{7}{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/state.json" "file_sha256" "83532cd8775da02f680d928be9e2f1005c5182bcc3e6f71ba99792efe1d70ef7"} {"question" "q-326437" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "89ff37eb35ec8c1ba12426d