/14067494/state.json" "file_sha256" "83532cd8775da02f680d928be9e2f1005c5182bcc3e6f71ba99792efe1d70ef7"} {"question" "q-327147" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write down the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for the given system:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A.$\n\n- We are given that the eigenvalues are\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=3\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=0.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1/2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=0,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=1$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(1) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4} \\\\ {e}^{4}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2{e}^{4} \\\\ {e}^{4}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{3}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ 2{e}^{3}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2{e}^{4} \\\\ {e}^{4}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{3}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=-2{e}^{4} \\\\ 2{e}^{3}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}={e}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the first equation from the second one, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{3}{c}_{1}=3{e}^{4}\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${c}_{1}=e$ into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{3}(e)+{c}_{2}={e}^{4}\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=-{e}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =e[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}-{e}^{4}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/state.json" "file_sha256" "83532cd8775da02f680d928be9e2f1005c5182bcc3e6f71ba99792efe1d70ef7"} {"question" "q-327147" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "89ff37eb35ec8c1ba12426d1ac9dd8c7ed420a07f95af35c6e904141d57a9a35"} {"question" "q-327147" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/state.json" "file_sha256" "83532cd8775da02f680d928be9e2f1005c5182bcc3e6f71ba99792efe1d70ef7"} {"question" "q-327147" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x(t)=-2{e}^{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}-3e[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "x(t)=2[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}-3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "x(t)=2e[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+{e}^{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "x(t)=2e[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}-3{e}^{4}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "x(t)=e[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}-{e}^{4}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/state.json" "file_sha256" "83532cd8775da02f680d928be9e2f1005c5182bcc3e6f71ba99792efe1d70ef7"} {"question" "q-327147" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x(t)=e[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}-{e}^{4}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/state.json" "file_sha256" "83532cd8775da02f680d928be9e2f1005c5182bcc3e6f71ba99792efe1d70ef7"} {"question" "q-324462" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the solution to the following initial value problem.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=4{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/state.json" "file_sha256" "83532cd8775da02f680d928be9e2f1005c5182bcc3e6f71ba99792efe1d70ef7"} {"question" "q-324462" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write down the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for the given system:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A.$\n\n- Since $A$ is upper-triangular, the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=4\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-1.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=4,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4(0)}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-0} & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} \\\\ 5{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}=-1 \\\\ 5{c}_{2}=10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${c}_{2}=2.$ Substituting into the first equation, we obtain ${c}_{1}=1.$ \n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+2[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/state.json" "file_sha256" "83532cd8775da02f680d928be9e2f1005c5182bcc3e6f71ba99792efe1d70ef7"} {"question" "q-324462" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "89ff37eb35ec8c1ba12426d1ac9dd8c7ed420a07f95af35c6e904141d57a9a35"} {"question" "q-324462" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/state.json" "file_sha256" "83532cd8775da02f680d928be9e2f1005c5182bcc3e6f71ba99792efe1d70ef7"} {"question" "q-324462" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x(t)=-[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+2[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}"] ["math" "x(t)=2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+3[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}"] ["math" "x(t)=2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}-[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}"] ["math" "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}"] ["math" "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+2[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/state.json" "file_sha256" "83532cd8775da02f680d928be9e2f1005c5182bcc3e6f71ba99792efe1d70ef7"} {"question" "q-324462" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+2[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/state.json" "file_sha256" "83532cd8775da02f680d928be9e2f1005c5182bcc3e6f71ba99792efe1d70ef7"} {"question" "e-21137" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the initial value problem\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/assets/c49d8a92f1fa8e94c78250d9b4aa771ef97e322603be667d3be98aa25c82cf81.png)\ngiven that the general solution to our differential equation is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/assets/e2e235f7970c2f644302c2f119837b14dc5e6f6a94969c53f92c0193f6aca629.png)\n\nThe solution to the initial value problem is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/assets/729acfdc97d8800458a057a419b8efdcb5d9087af3374125cfb293584e47394a.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/example-21137.json" "file_sha256" "857e0eb91e4786861d023c18efc10673f963d6fee275d947630d687351cabc71"} {"question" "e-21137" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the values of $c_1$ and $c_2$, we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}-{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}=7 \\\\ {c}_{1}-{c}_{2}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the second equation by $2$ and adding the result to the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n5c_1= 5 \n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nc_1 = 1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n1 - c_2 = -1\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nc_2 = 2.\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/example-21137.json" "file_sha256" "857e0eb91e4786861d023c18efc10673f963d6fee275d947630d687351cabc71"} {"question" "e-5609" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $\\lambda_1=3$ and $\\lambda_2=-2$ be eigenvalues of the $2\\times 2$ matrix  $A$ that correspond to eigenvectors \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/assets/15d8764ce12b985a991ca9a0704ba037ccc29fa488a3f23ee643688ceebae60c.png) \nGiven that the position of a particle at time $t$ seconds is given by the vector $\\mathbf{x}(t)$ and that $\\mathbf{x}(t)$ is the solution to the initial value problem\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/assets/88cd1c87501899a5d6b49ce3fd1678fe9485300a2da638fb496f2c80b298ea86.png)\nfind the position of the particle after $2$ seconds." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/example-5609.json" "file_sha256" "44e0e7e42db6f8cb0f9ab80d60086a9aeceff6adc2f139455f72554d8065f2d1"} {"question" "e-5609" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ has two distinct eigenvalues ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1}, {\\color{blue}\\lambda_2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1}$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2},$ the formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1{\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1} e^{{\\color{red}\\lambda_1} t} + c_2{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} e^{{\\color{blue}\\lambda_2} t}, \\qquad c_1,c_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our matrix differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the values of $c_1$ and $c_2,$ we substitute $t=0$ into the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}-11 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}-11 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}6{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ -2{c}_{1}-3{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-11 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}6{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=-11 \\\\ -2{c}_{1}-3{c}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $3$ and adding the result to the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n16c_1 = -32\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nc_1=-2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-2\\cdot(-2) -3c_2 = 1\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nc_2=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution for the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =-2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate $\\mathbf x(2)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(2) & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3\\cdot 2}+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2\\cdot 2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-12{e}^{6}+{e}^{-4} \\\\ 4{e}^{6}-3{e}^{-4}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/example-5609.json" "file_sha256" "44e0e7e42db6f8cb0f9ab80d60086a9aeceff6adc2f139455f72554d8065f2d1"} {"question" "e-5608" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the solution to the initial value problem\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/assets/e3f6d6e26983db7b894d0bb2ba09e66fdc55edecb0d9bc9d8a35821449707b7e.png)\n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1=2$ and $\\lambda_2=-3.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/example-5608.json" "file_sha256" "ef6284aefa549811a3e8ebeab19e8391f1e1dae7c547c10d7037e6a629c7b7bb"} {"question" "e-5608" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's construct the matrix differential equation $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)$ for the given system\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/assets/4790200622ff45c885147ca0c897eefcbb4bdcefe10d67b8960cc167c3b2cccf.png)\n\n- We are given that the eigenvalues are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1= 2\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\n\\lambda_2= -3.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For $\\lambda_1 =2,$ we get \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/assets/10bbcec99a48d331c21836dc104a1db2db241e50a534baf0db23c138168c5663.png)\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/assets/c2bff2faa79d780c5f89b28e72468c84a388d50e8c566b80e5d5677f90a5e3af.png)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2 =-3,$ we get \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/assets/704493d5ac4f48c88a9c521c4e43c41acfd80b6d6959237944fbc0d638e6012f.png)\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/assets/a77312d93a804e69dabb70aff5bc7bbf9798781c728dfc50fff1440da86b5b5c.png)\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the values of $c_1$ and $c_2,$ we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution and use the initial values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}+6{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=5 \\\\ {c}_{1}+6{c}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the first equation from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{c}_{2}=-5\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $c_2 = -1$ into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}-1=5\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =6[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/example-5608.json" "file_sha256" "ef6284aefa549811a3e8ebeab19e8391f1e1dae7c547c10d7037e6a629c7b7bb"} {"question" "q-39240" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}"] "category" "reviewed_ma_solution" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "804ef7823288082dae4a415e28ef3f5225fef22f37f3ca7fa53402ed405c2fb4" "reviewer_session" "01a118ea-3923-70c2-acd9-0830602bde5f" "rationale" "The original problem gives eigenpairs 1 with (1,-2) and -1 with (2,2). The initial condition yields c1+2c2=3 and -2c1+2c2=-3, hence c1=2 and c2=1/2. Therefore x(t)=(2,-4)e^t+(1,1)e^-t, original option 2. The original screenshot records successful grading and its worked solution derives this result. The stored bmatrix answer expresses exactly the same vectors and factors." "bindings" {"problem" "7df72384af52d49d44582bd19656bcab5739f3fddd2a08abc57936f2448e3ab1" "worked_solution" "034e5ed8d12594be52476f5250b6f0458133a5381690967e2436556d39bd7a80" "original_field" "7e106531a3c795eeba403195731b07c57dec7e3a2385830eebeccef42d353ede" "reviewed_field" "7e106531a3c795eeba403195731b07c57dec7e3a2385830eebeccef42d353ede" "raw_widget" "63f629812844b5b4cdce8c349901768c75a430415fd15dbc725adaae339954d4"} "evidence_files" [{"path" "q-39240-before.json" "sha256" "ee4d8aea59682266687e2c534efba6c288844f81e88d51302bec816e5a01cb38"} {"pa