"For A=[[4,1],[0,-1]], eigenpairs are 4 with (1,0) and -1 with (-1,5). The initial condition gives c1-c2=-1 and 5c2=10, so c1=1 and c2=2. Thus x(t)=(1,0)e^(4t)+2(-1,5)e^-t, original option 4. Successful grading and the authentic worked solution confirm it. The stored bmatrix expression is mathematically identical." "bindings" {"problem" "68f988597e873b91a385b2fc8a0e1cf59d4cb26b5b47fda432981cf96892b464" "worked_solution" "3b8e3ebc970a08d44cbac766e9b9c6d5392c108f2ab044f2818ca5b1d29a0958" "original_field" "4e328cc1e48e7c38829ef3e33bd960f48fac997956786d91fd5ebeadaaef200f" "reviewed_field" "4e328cc1e48e7c38829ef3e33bd960f48fac997956786d91fd5ebeadaaef200f" "raw_widget" "207427c44009211c003184bec501b413f831a7c6142b5a91cda64e20ba7e4239"} "evidence_files" [{"path" "q-324462-before.json" "sha256" "ba2e5ca5a6310dfaac6a845fe831bf9b00222acb05dcb640add98993823dc0f2"} {"path" "q-324462-before.png" "sha256" "039c44ac620254656fc93bcb307031b38c294bcbddbebb57434b78738f0025c9"} {"path" "q-324462-after.png" "sha256" "4211536a64609caf170ddb117d8060bf156b8c6133700e2d11f1aa6890ac8731"}]} {"question" "q-324462" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x(t)=-[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+2[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}"] ["math" "x(t)=2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+3[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}"] ["math" "x(t)=2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}-[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}"] ["math" "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}"] ["math" "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+2[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}"]] "category" "reviewed_ma_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "804ef7823288082dae4a415e28ef3f5225fef22f37f3ca7fa53402ed405c2fb4" "rationale" "For A=[[4,1],[0,-1]], eigenpairs are 4 with (1,0) and -1 with (-1,5). The initial condition gives c1-c2=-1 and 5c2=10, so c1=1 and c2=2. Thus x(t)=(1,0)e^(4t)+2(-1,5)e^-t, original option 4. Successful grading and the authentic worked solution confirm it. The stored bmatrix expression is mathematically identical."}] "usage" {"q-39240" [] "q-39241" [] "q-326002" [] "q-324462" [] "e-21137" [] "e-5609" [] "e-5608" []} "basis" 2258 "retractions" [[17592186134113 :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n$x^{′}(t) = Ax(t), x(0) = \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$\ngiven that the general solution to our differential equation is\n$x(t) = c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}e^{t} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}e^{-t}, c_{1}, c_{2} ∈ R$.\n\nThe solution to the initial value problem is\n\n| $x(t) =$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $e^{t} +$ |  | {{field-2}} |  | $e^{-t}$. |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | {{field-3}} |  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |  |"] [17592186134131 :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $2 \\cdot 2$ matrix with eigenvalues $λ_{1} = 0$ and $λ_{2} = 2$ corresponding to the eigenvectors $v_{1}$ and $v_{2}$ respectively, where\n\n$v_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, v_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$.\nGiven that the position of a particle at time $t$ seconds is given by the vector $x(t)$, and that $x(t)$ is the solution to the initial value problem\n$x^{′}(t) = Ax(t), x(0) = \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$,\nfind the position of the particle at $t = 5$ seconds."] [17592186134130 :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $2 \\cdot 2$ matrix with eigenvalues $λ_{1} = 1$ and $λ_{2} =-1$ corresponding to the eigenvectors $v_{1}$ and $v_{2}$ respectively, where\n\n$v_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}, v_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$.\nSolve the following initial value problem:\n$x^{′}(t) = Ax(t), x(0) = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186134130 :question/answer-fields 17592186131222] [17592186134111 :question/problem "Find the solution to the following initial value problem.\n\n${x_{1}^{′}(t) = 4x_{1}(t) + x_{2}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) =-x_{2}(t),x(0) = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186134111 :question/answer-fields 17592186131110] [17592186133460 :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n$\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$\ngiven that the general solution to our differential equation is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}[math]\\phantom{X \\mid}[/math] \\\\ [math]\\phantom{X \\mid}[/math]\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186133460 :question/worked-solution "To find the values of $c_1$ and $c_2$, we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(0) &= \\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nc_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{0} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}e^{0} &= \\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}3c_{1} + 2c_{2} \\\\ c_{1} - c_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{3c_{1} + 2c_{2} = 7; c_{1} - c_{2} =-1\n$$\n\nMultiplying the second equation by $2$ and adding the result to the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n5c_1= 5 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad c_1 = 1\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n1 - c_2 = -1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad c_2 = 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= 1 \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{t} + 2 \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}e^{-t} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{t} + \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}e^{-t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186133535 :question/problem "Let $\\lambda_1=3$ and $\\lambda_2=-2$ be eigenvalues of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ that correspond to eigenvectors\n$\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$\nGiven that the position of a particle at time $t$ seconds is given by the vector $\\mathbf{x}(t)$ and that $\\mathbf{x}(t)$ is the solution to the initial value problem\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-11 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nfind the position of the particle after $2$ seconds."] [17592186133535 :question/worked-solution "Given a system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ has two distinct eigenvalues ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1}, {\\color{blue}\\lambda_2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1}$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2}$, the formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1{\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1} e^{{\\color{red}\\lambda_1} t} + c_2{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} e^{{\\color{blue}\\lambda_2} t}, \\qquad c_1,c_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our matrix differential equation is\n\n$$\nx(t) = c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}e^{3t} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}e^{-2t}, c_{1}, c_{2} ∈ R\n$$\n\nNow, to find the values of $c_1$ and $c_2$, we substitute $t=0$ into the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(0) &= \\begin{bmatrix}-11 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nc_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}e^{0} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}e^{0} &= \\begin{bmatrix}-11 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}6c_{1} + c_{2} \\\\ -2c_{1} - 3c_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}-11 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{6c_{1} + c_{2} =-11; - 2c_{1} - 3c_{2} = 1\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $3$ and adding the result to the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n16c_1 = -32 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad c_1=-2\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-2\\cdot(-2) -3c_2 = 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad c_2=1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution for the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= -2 \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}e^{3t} + 1 \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}e^{-2t} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}e^{3t} + \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}e^{-2t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate $\\mathbf x(2)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(2) &= \\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}e^{3 \\cdot 2} + \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}e^{-2 \\cdot 2} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-12e^{6} + e^{-4} \\\\ 4e^{6} - 3e^{-4}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186133534 :question/problem "Find the solution to the initial value problem\n${x_{1}^{′}(t) = 3x_{1}(t) - x_{2}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) = 6x_{1}(t) - 4x_{2}(t)$\n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1=2$ and $\\lambda_2=-3$.*"] [17592186133534 :question/worked-solution "Let's construct the matrix differential equation\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nfor the given system\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ x_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n- We are given that the eigenvalues are\n$\\lambda_1= 2 \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \\lambda_2= -3$.\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.For $\\lambda_1 =2$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 6 & -6\\end{bmatrix}$\nSo, we get an eigenvector\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$For $\\lambda_2 =-3$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -1 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nSo, we get an eigenvector\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= c_{1}v_{1}e^{λ_{1}t} + c_{2}v_{2}e^{λ_{2}t} \\\\\n&= c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{2t} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}e^{-3t}, c_{1}, c_{2} ∈ R\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the values of $c_1$ and $c_2$, we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution and use the initial values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(0) &= \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nc_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{0} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}e^{0} &= \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}c_{1} + c_{2} \\\\ c_{1} + 6c_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{c_{1} + c_{2} = 5; c_{1} + 6c_{2} = 0\n$$\n\nSubtracting the first equation from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n5c_{2} =-5⟹c_{2} =-1\n$$\n\nSubstituting $c_2 = -1$ into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\nc_{1} - 1 = 5⟹c_{1} = 6\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= 6\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{2t} - \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}e^{-3t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186131111 17592186131112 17592186131113 17592186131114 17592186131115 17592186131223 17592186131224 17592186131225 17592186131226 17592186131227 17592186131229 17592186131230 17592186131231 17592186131232 17592186131233] "immutable_fields" [[17592186131110 :answer-field/correct] [17592186131110 :answer-field/id] [17592186131110 :answer-field/key] [17592186131110 :answer-field/type] [17592186131122 :answer-field/id] [17592186131122 :answer-field/key] [17592186131122 :answer-field/type] [17592186131123 :answer-field/id] [17592186131123 :answer-field/key] [17592186131123 :answer-field/type] [17592186131124 :answer-field/id] [17592186131124 :answer-field/key] [17592186131124 :answer-field/type] [17592186131125 :answer-field/id] [17592186131125 :answer-field/key] [17592186131125 :answer-field/type] [17592186131222 :answer-field/correct] [17592186131222 :answer-field/id] [17592186131222 :answer-field/key] [17592186131222 :answer-field/type] [17592186131228 :answer-field/correct] [17592186131228 :answer-field/id] [17592186131228 :answer-field/key] [17592186131228 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
